[BLAME] benchmark_4/parts/task_queue/pretense/task_queue_impl_pretense.py: The implementation pseudo-state machine is missing precondition gates (require calls) on its transition overrides for submit, dispatch, and complete, which allows Hypothesis to draw inputs that trigger spec-compliant contract violations: submit, dispatch, and complete must enforce require(self.state.is_available) before calling call_target, and dispatch must additionally enforce require(not self.state.is_empty), because the real TaskQueue implementation raises QueueUnavailableException when unavailable and QueueEmptyError when empty per the low-level specifications. Additionally, the get_outcome transition override executes an assert ret is not None that contradicts the spec postcondition permitting None returns for uncompleted tasks, and the state model lacks pending_count tracking to enable proper queue-empty precondition gating on dispatch.
[BLAME] benchmark_4/parts/task_queue/pretense/task_worker_impl_pretense.py: The implementation pseudo-state machine is missing precondition gates (require calls) on its execute transition override, which allows Hypothesis to draw inputs that trigger LifecycleResolutionError when the real TaskWorker.execute calls get_singleton(task_queue.TaskQueue) but TaskQueue is not registered in the test phase scope. The execute method must enforce require gates before calling call_target to prevent resolution errors on missing collaborator singletons, and the TestTaskWorkerImplReal test setUp calls only __initialize__ for TaskWorker without also initializing TaskQueue, so the pseudo-state machine must preemptively block execute transitions that would require TaskQueue availability rather than allowing them to crash at runtime.
